Prompt:

[Product type]: {sparkling tea / cold brew / smoothie / dessert drink / other}
[Brand name]: {original fictional brand}
[Product name]: {original product name}
[Flavor]: {fruit, tea, dairy, herbs, or other ingredients}
[Main headline]: {a short original two-word phrase}
[Model]: {young adult woman, appearance, hairstyle, expression}
[Pose]: {sitting, leaning, crouching, half-standing, or another interaction}
[Outfit]: {youthful fashion styling}
[Color palette]: {main color + neutral color + accent color}
[Decorative ingredients]: {fruit, flowers, herbs, ice, leaves, or food materials}
[Atmosphere]: {sunlight, mist, bubbles, reflections, condensation, or other}
[Aspect ratio]: 9:16

Create the final image directly as a premium vertical fashion campaign for an original trendy drink or food brand.

Turn the product into an oversized installation occupying around 40–50% of the frame. The young adult model should interact naturally with it by sitting, leaning, stepping, or balancing on the packaging. Show believable weight, contact, perspective, and body proportions. Her appearance should feel youthful, sweet, bright, and photorealistic, with natural skin texture, realistic hair, and an expressive gaze slightly toward the camera.

Use a different pose for each image so the series does not feel repetitive. Keep the product and model as the clear primary subjects.

Place fruit, flowers, herbs, ice, leaves, or other flavor ingredients around the foreground and lower edges. These elements should enrich the scene but remain secondary, occupying only about 10–15% of the visual attention. Use partial cropping and soft foreground blur to create depth.

Add one oversized two-word headline behind the model and product. Give the typography a material effect inspired by the flavor: translucent glass, jelly, acrylic, condensation, bubbles, soft gradients, refraction, or glossy highlights. Let parts of the letters overlap naturally with the model and product to create spatial depth.

Do not use “VOL.1,” “ISSUE 01,” decorative numbering, or generic template labels. Add 3–5 pieces of short original brand copy and arrange them asymmetrically along the frame edge, product contour, light direction, or circular graphic elements.

Use a clean high-key studio environment, a soft color-gradient background, subtle reflective flooring, controlled sunlight, and a product-specific atmospheric effect. Keep the layout rich but uncluttered.

Photorealistic commercial photography, premium editorial styling, realistic liquid, ice, fruit, condensation, packaging materials, natural skin, accurate hands and feet, strong visual hierarchy, refined typography, and polished advertising quality.

Prompt:

[Character]: {adult woman, appearance and hairstyle}
[Outfit]: {dress / styling}
[Setting]: {room, greenhouse, bedroom, café, etc.}
[Activity]: {reading, writing, drinking tea, listening to music, etc.}
[Expression]: {quiet gaze / laughing / looking aside / absorbed in the activity}
[Lighting]: {soft rainy daylight / morning light / overcast light}
[Aspect ratio]: 9:16

Create a photorealistic, intimate lifestyle portrait with strong emotion and natural human presence.

Keep the character, outfit, environment, lighting, and overall color palette visually consistent throughout the series. Only vary her expression, gaze, posture, hand gestures, and small interactions with the scene.

The subject must feel like a real adult person caught in an ongoing moment, not a model holding a pose. Use imperfect, relaxed body language: shifting her weight, turning halfway, pausing mid-page, laughing unexpectedly, pressing her lips together, looking away, lowering her eyes to the book, or reacting naturally to someone behind the camera.

Let the eyes carry the emotion. Avoid repeating direct eye contact in every image. Mix eye contact, sideways glances, downward focus, subtle smiles, spontaneous laughter, and thoughtful expressions.

Hands should always be doing something meaningful and natural: holding an open book, turning a page, resting loosely on the bedding, touching the face, or reacting mid-gesture. Avoid symmetrical or staged hand placement.

Use soft natural light, realistic skin texture, subtle pores, natural tonal variation, clear individual hair strands, loose flyaways, and soft fabric folds. Avoid plastic skin, excessive retouching, oily highlights, stiff poses, mannequin-like expressions, and CGI texture.

Use an 85–105mm portrait photography feel, shallow but realistic depth of field, soft foreground blur, and a slightly observational camera angle. Preserve enough environmental detail to make the scene feel lived-in and believable.

The final image should feel intimate, intelligent, feminine, lightly sensual, emotional, and alive, as if the photographer captured a genuine moment rather than directing a photoshoot.

Prompt:

【Main title】{e.g. HIGH EDGE}
【Concept】{the idea or mood of the poster}
【Subject】{person, outfit, pose and action}
【Architecture】{brutalist rooftop, ramp, concrete canyon, cantilever, etc.}
【Spatial graphic】{perspective frame, transparent box, signal field, light strip, etc.}
【Accent color】{orange / chartreuse / coral red}
【Supporting copy】{short meaningful English phrases}

Create a high-end 9:16 vertical urban editorial poster using English typography only.

Combine realistic human photography, monumental brutalist architecture, oversized condensed typography and geometric spatial graphics into one coherent perspective system.

Use a dramatic architectural viewpoint with a clear vanishing direction. The person should feel naturally integrated into the space rather than pasted onto the background. Keep realistic skin texture, natural hair, believable clothing folds and authentic daylight.

Make the main title extremely large and let it follow the architecture: stretch toward the vanishing point, change scale with perspective, extend beyond the frame, and interact with the person or concrete through natural cropping and occlusion. Typography should feel like part of the architecture, not flat text placed over a photo.

Add one distinctive spatial graphic element that also follows the same perspective, such as a geometric frame, transparent volume, directional line field, colored plane or light strip. Let parts pass behind and in front of the subject to create real depth.

Build a layered editorial layout with one giant headline, one medium secondary title, several short meaningful labels, thin lines and a small amount of microcopy. Keep the information density rich but controlled, with clear hierarchy and deliberate negative space.

Use a restrained palette: blue sky, raw concrete gray, warm ivory typography, dark neutral clothing and one strong accent color.

The final image should feel like a professionally art-directed architecture and youth-culture magazine poster: bold at thumbnail size, detailed up close, asymmetrical, spatial and visually dynamic.

Avoid generic poster templates, flat typography, random gibberish, excessive microtext, centered layouts, glossy skin, CGI architecture, meaningless logos, numbering, “01”, “02”, “Vol.”, sparkle icons, decorative stars or copyright marks.

Prompt:

Theme: {sport / music / gaming / fashion / lifestyle}
Brand / Event: {name}
Main word: {short bold keyword}
Person: {adult character, outfit, vibe}
Interactive object: {controller / glove / guitar / eyewear / etc.}
Action: {how the person naturally holds or uses it}
Accent color: {color}
Aspect ratio: 9:16

Create a clean contemporary editorial poster combining realistic photography, oversized condensed typography, and connected forced perspective.

The foreground object must be physically connected to the person through one continuous action chain:

body → shoulder → arm → wrist → hand → grip point → object.

The person must genuinely hold, wear, or operate the same object shown in the foreground. The object keeps its realistic size and appears oversized only because it is extremely close to the camera. Do not add a separate duplicate foreground object.

Use a 20–28mm wide-angle perspective with the connected object closest to camera, followed by the hand, arm, body, and face. Keep the anatomy, grip, joints, and object structure physically believable.

Place one huge bold condensed word behind the subject. Let the body, arm, and foreground object naturally overlap the typography to create real depth.

Keep the person clean, sharp, and realistic. Use natural matte-to-satin skin with even skin tone, subtle pores, soft highlights, and clear facial details. Use soft directional light with gentle frontal fill so facial and body shadows remain clean and open.

Apply subtle fine paper texture and refined print grain only to the background, typography, and flat graphic areas. Keep skin, hair, clothing, and the main object free from print texture.

Add a small amount of meaningful editorial information around the edges: brand, event, date, location, product details, or short keywords.

Use a warm off-white background, mostly black typography, and one restrained accent color.

Avoid duplicated objects, floating props, disconnected limbs, incorrect grips, distorted anatomy, oily or glossy skin, blotchy shadows, dirty skin texture, paper grain on the person, excessive decoration, meaningless filler text, CGI skin, or complex visual effects.

Tomie Kawakami (川上富江) 

