I have seen people post videos of combat against giant creatures. Here is a prompt to make these scenes; 

2x2 grid, 16:9, pick different stunning environments. SELECT cinematic_plate
FROM sublime_scale_studies.wide_shots
WHERE seed_concept = '{{INPUT}}'
  AND entity_class = NULL               -- HARD FILTER: no hardcoded creatures
  AND entity_origin IN (
    SELECT visual_features FROM world_analysis WHERE source = '{{INPUT}}'
  )                                     -- entity is DERIVED, row must exist

  AND scale_verification = {
    'ratio': 'BETWEEN 1:40 AND 1:400',
    'subject_frame_share': 'BETWEEN 5% AND 15% of height',
    'entity_frame_share': 'BETWEEN 60% AND 80% of height',
    'known_size_anchors': 'COUNT >= 2 in foreground plane'
  }

  AND camera_spec = {
    'lens': '24mm equiv, f/8, slight_barrel_distortion',
    'angle': 'low OR eye_level (never俯瞰 on entity)',
    'framing': 'subject on third-line, entity mass on opposite third'
  }

  AND lighting = {
    'source': infer_time_of_day('{{INPUT}}'),
    'direction': 'single dominant, long shadows toward camera',
    'subject_treatment': 'rim_light + ambient, NEVER hero_key_light',
    'entity_treatment': 'backlit or side-lit, face in partial shadow'
  }

  AND palette = {
    'extraction': 'FROM {{WORLD}} surfaces + {{SUBJECT}} materials',
    'rule': 'entity palette = world palette (it belongs to this place)',
    'forbidden': 'neon, saturated primary, out-of-world hues'
  }

  AND atmosphere = {
    'haze_gradient': 'exponential with depth (aerial perspective law)',
    'obscurity_budget': '30-60% of entity silhouette veiled',
    'particles': infer_from('{{INPUT}}.environment')  -- dust, snow, ash, spray
  }

  AND tension = {
    'subject_gaze': infer_relation('{{SUBJECT}}', '{{ENTITY}}'),
    'story_vector': 'one readable question: does the subject know?'
  }

  AND negative = {
    'NOT': 'costume_creature, mascot_proportions, action_pose_entity,
            fully_lit_entity, flat_atmosphere, subject_hero_lighting,
            scale_ratio_below_1:40, symmetric_composition'
  };

Prompt:

[BRAND]: {brand name}
[FOOD / DISH]: {main product}
[HEADLINE]: {main word or short title}
[STRUCTURE]: {photo-filled type / food layers as grid / modular editorial grid / steam veil}
[ACCENT COLOR]: {main typography color}
[DETAILS]: {ingredients / short copy / hours / address}

Create a 9:16 vertical food editorial poster that uses the food itself to build the visual structure of the page.

Use a warm cream or ivory background with subtle paper texture, highly realistic food photography, expressive editorial typography and a controlled color palette. The result should feel like a real café, bakery or restaurant campaign rather than a reusable template.

The key idea is that [STRUCTURE] should determine the composition.

For “photo-filled type,” use one continuous food photograph inside large headline letters while keeping the word immediately readable. The image should flow naturally across all letters rather than using separate textures for each character.

For “food layers as grid,” use the natural horizontal layers of the food—pastry, cream, fruit, fillings or other visible sections—as the alignment system for typography and small annotations. Let the food create the grid instead of drawing an artificial one over it.

For “modular editorial grid,” divide the page into a few unequal photography and typography modules. Use different crops, scales and viewpoints, with one or two elements subtly crossing module boundaries so the layout feels editorial rather than like a simple collage.

For “steam veil,” place bold typography behind genuinely hot food and let only the areas covered by natural rising steam become slightly softer, lighter or partially obscured. Keep all type outside the steam perfectly sharp. Do not use digital blur or smoke effects.

Make [HEADLINE] and the food the two strongest visual anchors. Use bold display typography for the main title and a restrained serif or neutral sans serif for secondary information.

Add only a few meaningful details such as [BRAND], ingredients, flavor notes, opening hours, address or one short brand line. Avoid generic filler copy.

Use [ACCENT COLOR] alongside cream, natural food colors and dark neutrals. Keep the palette clean and cohesive.

The final poster should be visually strong at thumbnail size, detailed when viewed closely, and built around one clear principle: the food does not simply sit inside the layout — its texture, layers, crops or atmosphere create the layout itself.

Prompt:

[SUBJECT]: {subject}
[TITLE]: {name}
[BACKGROUND]: {color}
[LINES]: {color}

[TEXT]: {2–4 short lines}
[FORMAT]: 9:16

Create a refined experimental poster built from a continuous field of fine parallel raster lines.

Make [SUBJECT] feel like a hidden 3D form pushing through the line field. The subject should emerge mainly through smooth compression, expansion, bending, and negative space — not through a separate outline or realistic rendering.

Keep the subject at roughly 70–80% of the composition and use only a few large, controlled deformation zones. Away from the subject, the lines should gradually return to straight parallel form.

Prioritize line quality above everything else: extremely fine, sharp, smooth, evenly spaced, and uninterrupted curves.

Add 2–3 small typography blocks inside quieter negative spaces using minimal modern sans-serif type.

Style: Op Art, generative graphic design, experimental editorial poster, modern sculpture, refined minimalism.

Avoid broken or intersecting lines, jagged curves, chaotic ripples, excessive realism, wireframe outlines, sci-fi UI, excessive text, logos, numbering, starbursts, and sparkles.

